});

document.getElementById('decadeTable').innerHTML='<tr><th>Decade</th><th>Ceremonies</th><th>Unique Winners</th><th>Repeat Wins</th><th>Repeat Share</th></tr>'+dec.map(d=>`<tr><td>${d.Decade}s</td><td>${d.ceremonies}</td><td>${d.unique_winners}</td><td>${d.repeat_wins}</td><td>${d.repeat_share.toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>`).join('');
document.getElementById('decadeNarrative').textContent=`Diversity peaks in the ${data.decade_most_unique}s (all 10 ceremonies won by different composers). Repeat concentration is highest in the 1940s (30.0%) and elevated again in the partial 2020s (28.6%), suggesting alternating cycles between consolidation and discovery.`;

document.getElementById('eraText').innerHTML=`
<p><span class='hl'>Golden Age (1934–1959):</span> Early decades show academy preference coalescing around recurring orchestral voices while still onboarding many first-time winners. The 1940s and 1950s have moderate repeat concentration (30% and 20%), indicating prestige clusters around a limited but not closed composer elite.</p>
<p><span class='hl'>Silver Age (1960–1979):</span> Repeat rates stabilize at 20% in both decades, with 8 unique winners per decade. This suggests a balanced period: enough repeat recognition to reward established craft, but regular turnover to admit new stylistic directions.</p>
<p><span class='hl'>Modern Era (1980–1999):</span> The 1980s are the strongest diversity decade in the entire series (10 unique winners in 10 ceremonies), followed by a 1990s re-concentration (20% repeat share), including a two-year consecutive streak by Alan Menken (1991–1992).</p>
<p><span class='hl'>Contemporary Era (2000–2026):</span> The 2000s remain diverse (90% unique), while the 2010s return to 20% repeat share. In the 2020s (through 2026), repeat wins rise to 28.6%, driven partly by Ludwig Göransson's rapid accumulation, including consecutive wins (2023–2024) and another in 2026.</p>`;

const streakList=data.streaks.map(s=>`<li>${s[0]}: ${s[1]}–${s[2]} (${s[3]} consecutive wins)</li>`).join('');
document.getElementById('milestones').innerHTML=`
<ul>
<li><b>Most recent winner:</b> ${data.latest.Composer} for <i>${data.latest.Film}</i> (${data.latest.Year}).</li>
<li><b>Longest gap between wins:</b> ${data.longest_gap.composer}, ${data.longest_gap.gap_years} years (${data.longest_gap.from} to ${data.longest_gap.to}).</li>
<li><b>Longest consecutive-win streaks:</b><ul>${streakList}</ul></li>
<li><b>Top-win record:</b> ${data.max_wins} wins, shared by ${data.max_composers.join(', ')}.</li>
<li><b>Data limitation note:</b> youngest/oldest winner ages are not derivable from this CSV because no birthdate/age field is provided.</li>
</ul>`;

document.getElementById('conclusions').innerHTML=`
<ul>
<li>Oscar score history is neither fully concentrated nor fully diffuse: repeat winners account for ${data.repeat_rate.toFixed(1)}% of ceremonies, while 69 unique composers still emerge across 93 years.</li>
<li>The ${data.decade_most_unique}s mark a clear diversification crest, contrasting with consolidation decades like the 1940s and current 2020s-to-date.</li>
<li>The all-time leaderboard is unusually plural: three-way tie at four wins prevents a single canonical "Oscar score monarch."</li>
<li>Recent years show fast-cycle recognition for contemporary composers; Ludwig Göransson's four wins by 2026 signal accelerated modern canon formation.</li>
<li>Heading into the late 2020s, the key trend to watch is whether repeat concentration in the 2020s persists (entrenchment) or reverts toward 2000s-style diversification.</li>
</ul>`;
</script>
</body>
</html>
